  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =12-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}=0\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no real solutions, so there are no points of inflection.",
